Q: Subclassing String in JavaScript A: <p>Starting in ES2015 you can do this:</p>
<pre><code>class FullName extends String{
    splitName() {
        const space = this.indexOf(' ');
        return {first: this.slice(0, space), last: this.slice(space+1)};
    }
}

var value = new FullName('PHP Guru');
value.splitName()//{first: 'PHP', last: 'Guru'}
</code></pre>
<p><code>new FullName()</code> creates a String object with the following:</p>
<pre><code>typeof value=='object' // true
Object.prototype.toString.call(value) // '[object String]'
value instanceof String // true
value.constructor.name // 'FullName'
value instanceof FullName // true
</code></pre>
<p><code>new FullName()</code> does not construct a subclass of String. It constructs a String object that inherits from a plain prototype object containing your properties and methods. And said plain prototype object inherits from <code>String.prototype</code>.</p>
<p>Under the hood it looks something like this (ES5 equivalent):</p>
<pre><code>// define FullName constructor
function FullName(value) {
    return Object.setPrototypeOf(new String(arguments.length? value: ''), FullName.prototype);
}
// define FullName prototype object
FullName.prototype = Object.create(String.prototype, {
    splitName: {value: function splitName() {
        var space = this.indexOf(' ');
        return {first: this.slice(0, space), last: this.slice(space+1)};
    }}
    ,
    constructor: {value: FullName}
});
// Make FullName inherit from String (methods such as `fromCharCode()`)
Object.setPrototypeOf(FullName, String);


// Now the following works the same as above.
var value = new FullName('PHP Guru');
value.splitName()//{first: 'PHP', last: 'Guru'}
typeof value=='object' // true
Object.prototype.toString.call(value) // '[object String]'
value instanceof String // true
value.constructor.name // 'FullName'
value instanceof FullName // true
</code></pre>
<p>As you can see <code>FullName()</code> still returns a String object. Its prototype chain is just modified to include the new properties and methods without mutating <code>String.prototype</code>.</p>

Q: Is there a way to play background music using Google Apps Script? A: <p>I used the answer to this question as a starting point: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/26897862">playing sound with google script</a></p>
<p>You would need to open a html sidebar and use an audio tag, to do this you can use the HtmlService class [1].</p>
<p>As a total background you can't, the sidebar must be always open to play the music. But you could still play the audio while editing the document.</p>
<p>To add the audio setting you can add the <code>controls</code> attribute to the audio tag [2]. For playing the audio automatically you can add the <code>autoplay</code> attibute [3].</p>
<p>Here is the code I implemented to achieve your goal. The code gets the selected value and uses it to change the <code>autoplay</code> value to true and to display the audio as well. Also, when the <code>select</code> element is on focus, it gets the previous selected value so later (when a new value is selected) it can be used to stop the previous audio selection and not display it anymore. For these purposes I used the <code>onchange</code> [4] and the <code>onfocus</code> [5] events.</p>
<p><strong>Code.gs</strong></p>
<pre><code>var SIDEBAR_TITLE = 'Sidebar Musicbox';

function onOpen(e) {
  DocumentApp.getUi()
  .createMenu('Custom Menu')
  .addItem('Show sidebar', 'showSidebar')
  .addToUi();
}

function showSidebar() {
  var ui = HtmlService.createHtmlOutputFromFile('Sidebar')
  .setSandboxMode(HtmlService.SandboxMode.IFRAME)
  .setTitle(SIDEBAR_TITLE);
  DocumentApp.getUi().showSidebar(ui);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Sidebar.html</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
   &lt;head&gt;
      &lt;base target=&quot;_top&quot;&gt;
   &lt;/head&gt;
   &lt;body&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;sidebar branding-below&quot;&gt;
         &lt;p&gt;
            A little music for your enjoyment!
         &lt;/p&gt;
         &lt;form&gt;
            &lt;select id=&quot;music&quot; onchange=&quot;playSelection();&quot; onfocus=&quot;setOldValue(this.value);&quot;&gt;
               &lt;option value=&quot;0&quot;&gt;0&lt;/option&gt;
               &lt;option value=&quot;1&quot;&gt;1&lt;/option&gt;
               &lt;option value=&quot;2&quot;&gt;2&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;/select&gt;
         &lt;/form&gt;
         &lt;br&gt; 
         &lt;audio id=&quot;player0&quot; controls style=&quot;display:none&quot;&gt;
            &lt;source src=&quot;[WEB-URL-FOR-MP3-FILE]&quot; type=&quot;audio/mpeg&quot;&gt;
            Your browser does not support the audio element.
         &lt;/audio&gt;
         &lt;audio id=&quot;player1&quot; controls style=&quot;display:none&quot;&gt;
            &lt;source src=&quot;[WEB-URL-FOR-MP3-FILE]&quot; type=&quot;audio/mpeg&quot;&gt;
            Your browser does not support the audio element.
         &lt;/audio&gt;
         &lt;audio id=&quot;player2&quot; controls style=&quot;display:none&quot;&gt;
            &lt;source src=&quot;[WEB-URL-FOR-MP3-FILE]&quot; type=&quot;audio/mpeg&quot;&gt;
            Your browser does not support the audio element.
         &lt;/audio&gt;
         &lt;br&gt; 
         &lt;div id=&quot;sidebar-status&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div class=&quot;sidebar bottom&quot;&gt;
         &lt;span class=&quot;gray branding-text&quot;&gt;Docs Add-on Sound Demo&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
   &lt;/body&gt;
   &lt;script&gt;
      var previousValue;

      //Function called when select onFocus     
      function setOldValue(e) {
        previousValue = e;
      }

      //Function called when selected value change     
      function playSelection() {
        //Get the value for the selected option
        var selectedValue = document.getElementById(&quot;music&quot;).value;

        //Latest and previous selection IDs 
        var player = &quot;player&quot; + selectedValue;
        var previousPlayer = &quot;player&quot; + previousValue;

        //Stop and don't display the previous selection of audio
        document.getElementById(previousPlayer).style.display = &quot;none&quot;;
        document.getElementById(previousPlayer).autoplay = false;
        document.getElementById(previousPlayer).load();

        //Play and display the new selection and put the focus on it
        document.getElementById(player).style.display = &quot;block&quot;;
        document.getElementById(player).autoplay = true;
        document.getElementById(player).load();
        document.getElementById(player).focus();
      }   
   &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>[1] <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/html/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/html/</a></p>
<p>[2] <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/tags/att_audio_controls.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3schools.com/tags/att_audio_controls.asp</a></p>
<p>[3] <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/tags/att_audio_autoplay.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3schools.com/tags/att_audio_autoplay.asp</a></p>
<p>[4] <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/event_onchange.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/event_onchange.asp</a></p>
<p>[5] <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/event_onfocus.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3schools.com/jsref/event_onfocus.asp</a></p>

Q: Accessing struct members via pointer is not constant expression? A: <p>Other answers explain the issue with your current approach. However, you don't have to rely on specific layout of the class to use offsets into the class to access members. Member pointers are, under the hood, basically just offsets into the class:</p>
<pre><code>struct B {
    double value{1};
};

struct C {
    double value{2};
};

struct A {
    B b;
    C c;
};

template &lt;typename T&gt;
constexpr double h(const A&amp; a, T A::* ptr)
{
    return (a.*ptr).value;
}

static_assert([] {
    A a{};
    if (h(a,&amp;A::b) != 1) return false;
    if (h(a,&amp;A::c) != 2) return false;
    return true;
}());
</code></pre>
<p>As <code>b</code> and <code>c</code> are of different type, the respective member pointers are of different type too. You'd need to add some type erasure to establish a mapping from enum / integer to the member pointers.</p>

Q: What is the purpose of std::meta::variable_of? A: <p>A function parameter and a parameter variable are two different things and they are reflected differently.</p>
<p>A function parameter is something that interests a caller, and a parameter variable is something that only makes sense from the callee's perspective. You can access the parameters if you just have a function prototype, but the variables are only visible from the function body. They may have different types, as top-level qualifiers are removed from the parameter type, but not from the variable type. They also have different display strings.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;print&gt;
#include &lt;meta&gt;

void foo(const int x)
{
    std::print (&quot;foo perspective\n&quot;);
    constexpr auto var_info = ^^x;
    constexpr auto type_info = std::meta::type_of(var_info);
    std::print(&quot;{} : {}\n&quot;, std::meta::display_string_of(var_info), std::meta::display_string_of(type_info));

    constexpr auto params_info = std::define_static_array(std::meta::parameters_of(^^foo));
    constexpr auto param_info = params_info[0];
    constexpr auto var2_info = std::meta::variable_of(param_info);
    static_assert(var_info == var2_info);
    constexpr auto type2_info = std::meta::type_of(param_info);
    std::print(&quot;{} : {}\n&quot;, std::meta::display_string_of(param_info), std::meta::display_string_of(type2_info));
}

int main()
{
    std::print (&quot;main perspective\n&quot;);
    constexpr auto params_info = std::define_static_array(std::meta::parameters_of(^^foo));
    constexpr auto param_info = params_info[0];
    constexpr auto type_info = std::meta::type_of(param_info);
    std::print(&quot;{} : {}\n&quot;, std::meta::display_string_of(param_info), std::meta::display_string_of(type_info));
    // does not compile: constexpr auto var_info = std::meta::variable_of(param_info);
    foo(42);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>main perspective
&lt;parameter x of void foo(int)&gt; : int
foo perspective
x : const int
&lt;parameter x of void foo(int)&gt; : int

</code></pre>

Q: Get the playPause function to switch icons in the browser A: <p>As explained by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/80001304/15015675"><em>TKDKid1000</em></a> it is recommended to <strong>separate playback logic from the presentation</strong> (so icon toggling).</p>
<p>I would also recommend to avoid additional icon replacements.
Instead you can load both FontAwesome icons (for play and pause) at once while hiding the pause icon by default.</p>
<p>Once you click on &quot;play&quot; – the &quot;pause&quot; button is displayed and the &quot;play&quot; button is hidden.</p>
<p>This concept works for both SVG and icon-font usage.<br />
Modern FontAwesome versions provide both flavors.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let song = document.getElementById("song");
let btnPlay = document.getElementById("btnPlay");

// playback logic
btnPlay.addEventListener('click',(e)=&gt;{
    if (song.paused) {
      song.play(); 
    }else {
      song.pause();
    }
});

// player icon presentation
let evts = ['play', 'pause'];
evts.forEach(evt=&gt;{
  song.addEventListener(evt, (e)=&gt;{
    if(evt==='play'){
      btnPlay.classList.add("btn-playing");
    }else{
      btnPlay.classList.remove("btn-playing");
    }
  });
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.controls {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  gap: 1em;
}

.btn{
  background:none;
  border:none;
  appearance:none;
}

/* hide pause button */
.btn-play-pause{
  .fa-pause{
    display:none;
  }
}

/* show pause button */
.btn-playing{
  .fa-play{
    display:none;
  }
  .fa-pause{
    display:block;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/7.3.1/js/all.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div class="container"&gt;
  &lt;div class="music-player"&gt;
    &lt;audio id="song"&gt;
      &lt;source src="https://www.soundhelix.com/examples/mp3/SoundHelix-Song-1.mp3" type="audio/mpeg"&gt;
    &lt;/audio&gt;

    &lt;input type="range" value="0" id="progress"&gt;
    &lt;div class="controls"&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-back"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-backward"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button id="btnPlay" class="btn btn-play-pause"&gt;
        &lt;!-- icons --&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-play"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-pause"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-forward"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-forward"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Example with icon font icon-injection (via <code>:before</code> pseudo)</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let song = document.getElementById("song");
let btnPlay = document.getElementById("btnPlay");

// playback logic
btnPlay.addEventListener('click',(e)=&gt;{
    if (song.paused) {
      song.play(); 
    }else {
      song.pause();
    }
});

// player icon presentation
let evts = ['play', 'pause'];
evts.forEach(evt=&gt;{
  song.addEventListener(evt, (e)=&gt;{
    if(evt==='play'){
      btnPlay.classList.add("btn-playing");
    }else{
      btnPlay.classList.remove("btn-playing");
    }
  });
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.controls {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  gap: 1em;
}

.btn{
  background:none;
  border:none;
  appearance:none;
}

/* hide pause button */
.btn-play-pause{
  .fa-pause{
    display:none;
  }
}

/* show pause button */
.btn-playing{
  .fa-play{
    display:none;
  }
  .fa-pause{
    display:block;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!--
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/7.3.1/js/all.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
--&gt;
&lt;link href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/7.3.1/css/all.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;

&lt;div class="container"&gt;
  &lt;div class="music-player"&gt;
    &lt;audio id="song"&gt;
      &lt;source src="https://www.soundhelix.com/examples/mp3/SoundHelix-Song-1.mp3" type="audio/mpeg"&gt;
    &lt;/audio&gt;

    &lt;input type="range" value="0" id="progress"&gt;
    &lt;div class="controls"&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-back"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-backward"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button id="btnPlay" class="btn btn-play-pause"&gt;
        &lt;!-- icons --&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-play"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-pause"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-forward"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-forward"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>In the above examples we're simply toggling play/pause icons solely by CSS display.</p>
<p><code>&lt;i id=&quot;ctrlIcon&quot; class=&quot;fa-solid fa-play&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;</code> should rather be for presentation alone and not your click target. Instead you should use <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> elements.</p>
<h3><code>:playing</code> and <code>:paused</code> pseudo selectors</h3>
<p>As commented by <em>Yogi</em> we may also toggle visibility via <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:playing" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>:playing</code></a> and <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/:paused" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>:paused</code></a> CSS pseudo selectors – making the JavaScript class toggling obsolete. As of writing this post <strong>browser support is still spotty</strong> (2026).</p>
<p>The following snippet works fine in Frefox but fails in Chromium/Blink based browsers.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let song = document.getElementById("song");
let btnPlay = document.getElementById("btnPlay");

// playback logic
btnPlay.addEventListener('click',(e)=&gt;{
    if (song.paused) {
      song.play(); 
    }else {
      song.pause();
    }
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.controls {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  gap: 1em;
}

.btn{
  background:none;
  border:none;
  appearance:none;
}

/* hide pause button */
.music-player
.btn-play-pause{
  .fa-pause{
    display:none;
  }
}

/* target player instances with playing media element */
.music-player:has(audio:playing){
   .fa-play{
    display:none;
  }
  .fa-pause{
    display:block;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/7.3.1/js/all.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div class="container"&gt;
  &lt;div class="music-player"&gt;
    &lt;audio id="song"&gt;
      &lt;source src="https://www.soundhelix.com/examples/mp3/SoundHelix-Song-1.mp3" type="audio/mpeg"&gt;
    &lt;/audio&gt;

    &lt;input type="range" value="0" id="progress"&gt;

    &lt;div class="controls"&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-back"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-backward"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button id="btnPlay" class="btn btn-play-pause"&gt;
        &lt;!-- icons --&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-play"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-pause"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-forward"&gt;
        &lt;i class="fa-solid fa-forward"&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>So in 2026 you should prefer the previous class toggling via JS.</p>
